1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:07,054
Tomorrow's surgical schedule is full. Inventory is
spread across the warehouse, hospitals, and sales reps.

2
00:00:07,357 --> 00:00:12,400
Here's how Tracewell helps the team see what
needs attention, and act before the deadline.

3
00:00:13,306 --> 00:00:18,726
It's nine in the morning. Avery, the
operations manager, opens tomorrow's cases.

4
00:00:18,746 --> 00:00:23,248
Five are scheduled, each shown
in its own hospital's local time.

5
00:00:23,551 --> 00:00:26,983
The first one needs attention. It's a confirmed shortage.

6
00:00:27,003 --> 00:00:30,848
The case needs two tibial inserts,
and only one is available.

7
00:00:31,151 --> 00:00:36,332
The issue has an owner, Dana, the rep on the
case. And a deadline: three o'clock today.

8
00:00:36,949 --> 00:00:43,143
The second case isn't short. The courier recorded
the delivery, but the hospital hasn't checked it in.

9
00:00:43,163 --> 00:00:45,385
Marcus, at Harlan, can confirm it.

10
00:00:45,688 --> 00:00:50,873
And this is what ready looks like. Delivered,
and checked in by the hospital's own staff.

11
00:00:51,778 --> 00:00:56,235
Back to the shortage. Five on hand.
Four reserved for other cases.

12
00:00:56,255 --> 00:00:59,050
One available. Two required. Short one.

13
00:01:01,416 --> 00:01:08,269
Every number has evidence behind it. The on-hand export,
a physical count from this morning, and the reservations,

14
00:01:08,289 --> 00:01:09,584
with who made them.

15
00:01:09,886 --> 00:01:14,714
One click opens the exact spreadsheet
row the coordinator loaded on Friday.

16
00:01:15,619 --> 00:01:20,689
So where is another one? Avery opens the
case, and searches for the exact item.

17
00:01:20,709 --> 00:01:21,825
No substitutes.

18
00:01:22,127 --> 00:01:26,279
One unit qualifies. It is in the
trunk stock of Chris, another rep.

19
00:01:26,299 --> 00:01:31,385
It was counted this morning. And it can
reach the hospital well before the cutoff.

20
00:01:31,405 --> 00:01:34,481
The travel time is an estimate, and it says so.

21
00:01:35,452 --> 00:01:39,459
The four warehouse units are
excluded, and the reason is on screen.

22
00:01:39,479 --> 00:01:41,853
They're reserved for two later cases.

23
00:01:42,156 --> 00:01:45,520
Before committing, a preview
shows which cases are affected.

24
00:01:46,476 --> 00:01:49,803
One click reserves the unit and creates the transfer.

25
00:01:49,823 --> 00:01:54,710
The status is honest. Planned. Nothing
picked, dispatched, or received yet.

26
00:01:55,012 --> 00:02:00,124
Back on the board, the transfer is waiting
on Chris, with a time it has to leave by.

27
00:02:00,144 --> 00:02:03,776
And the case is still one unit short. Nobody is guessing.

28
00:02:05,678 --> 00:02:10,594
Avery can also just ask. Which cases
need my attention before three today?

29
00:02:13,339 --> 00:02:19,951
The answer comes from the same records as the board.
Three items, in deadline order, each with its owner.

30
00:02:19,971 --> 00:02:24,941
In this recording the assistant runs in
demo mode, so the wording is templated.

31
00:02:24,961 --> 00:02:26,437
The numbers are live.

32
00:02:27,531 --> 00:02:31,257
Every answer links to its
sources. This one opens the case.

33
00:02:32,455 --> 00:02:38,088
A follow-up, without repeating the case number.
Why is this case short if we have five on hand?

34
00:02:40,033 --> 00:02:45,132
Same arithmetic as the board, already
updated for the unit Avery just reserved.

35
00:02:46,037 --> 00:02:52,096
In the field, Dana has her cases on her phone. This
morning's case is finished, so she closes it out.

36
00:02:52,399 --> 00:02:56,120
She marks what happened, and scans
the label. For this recording,

37
00:02:56,140 --> 00:02:59,283
the scan comes from a simulator instead of a camera.

38
00:02:59,587 --> 00:03:03,779
Each scan is confirmed by the server,
with the lot read from the barcode.

39
00:03:04,083 --> 00:03:09,349
When every item is accounted for, she submits
the closeout, and finance is notified.

40
00:03:10,530 --> 00:03:15,132
Back at the office, that case now shows
completed, with exactly what was used.

41
00:03:15,152 --> 00:03:17,969
The item, the lot, who recorded it, and when.

42
00:03:20,769 --> 00:03:27,470
That lot number matters. This morning, a manufacturer
notice arrived for one lot of femoral components.

43
00:03:27,490 --> 00:03:30,838
It was entered, then verified by a second person.

44
00:03:31,141 --> 00:03:35,187
The trace finds every unit of
that lot. One in the warehouse.

45
00:03:35,207 --> 00:03:39,119
One in transit. One at a
hospital. Two of tomorrow's cases.

46
00:03:39,139 --> 00:03:42,888
And the unit implanted this morning, from Dana's scan.

47
00:03:44,788 --> 00:03:48,693
Activating the recall places
holds, and gives each owner a task.

48
00:03:51,187 --> 00:03:56,942
And tomorrow's board flags both affected cases right
away, while there is still time to replace them.

49
00:03:59,559 --> 00:04:05,890
One place to see tomorrow's cases, the evidence
behind every answer, and who needs to act by when.

50
00:04:06,193 --> 00:04:09,197
Let's walk through your inventory and upcoming cases.

